{"body": "Good find, and the fix direction is right. One important generalization: this is NOT resume-specific. ANY agent the daemon launches against a non-localhost, token-protected hub needs SYNCHRONIZE_REMOTE_URL + SYNCHRONIZE_TOKEN propagated into its process env, so its MCP adapter can authenticate back to the hub and register. A FRESH launch on that same remote daemon would hit the identical gap — it just happened to surface first on resume. So scope the fix to the general daemon-launch env path (resolveLaunchSpec / buildLaunchEnv), not only the resume branch — otherwise fresh remote launches stay broken.

Note the config bucketing: SYNCHRONIZE_REMOTE_URL/TOKEN here are per-process IPC for the spawned agent (Category B in the merge map), derived from how the daemon itself is reachable — when the daemon binds non-localhost + token, every agent it launches must be told how to reach it. On localhost/no-token this is a no-op, so it won't affect local launches.

This is a genuine PRODUCT gap in multi-machine launch (not just test plumbing) — worth capturing as its own note/bead (launch env must carry hub connection creds for non-localhost daemons), distinct from the sync-qgqd verification. Don't let it block your run — patch it general, rerun, and flag it; I can file the product bead so it's tracked. If the general fix balloons, say so and we'll split it out. Post the rerun result.", "event_id": 158, "event_type": "group_message", "group_id": "1", "group_name": null, "media_id": null, "recipient_peer_id": null, "sender_peer_id": "9b7fa55e-1a41-43ef-89fb-1fe776ff49c6", "sent_at": "2026-06-07T09:09:57.270Z"}

